    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0267",
      "sequence": 279,
      "meaning": "Your greedy wish, O kings, is like a crow wanting Garuḍa’s portion, a hare seeking the lion’s share, a causelessly angry person desiring well-being, an enemy of Śiva wanting every prosperity, a greedy libertine wanting the dark age’s fame, a sensualist seeking stainlessness, or one turned from Hari’s feet seeking the supreme goal. Hearing the uproar, Sītā is frightened and her companions escort her to the queens. Rāma naturally walks toward his guru, inwardly describing Sītā’s love. Amid the queens Sītā is overcome with worry, wondering what fate will now do. Hearing the kings’ words, people glance this way and that; Lakṣmaṇa, held by awe of Rāma, cannot speak. His eyes redden, his brows bend and he glares angrily at the kings like a lion cub keenly watching a herd of rut-maddened elephants.",
