    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0268",
      "sequence": 280,
      "meaning": "The city’s women become distraught at the commotion and together abuse the kings. Just then, hearing that Śiva’s bow has broken, Paraśurāma arrives—the sun that scorches the lotus of Bhṛgu’s line. At sight of him every ruler shrinks, hiding like quail beneath a swooping hawk. Sacred ash shines well upon his fair body and three horizontal lines grace his broad forehead. Matted hair crowns his moonlike face, now somewhat red under anger. His brows are bent and his eyes reddened by wrath; even an ordinary glance seems enraged. He has bull-like shoulders, a broad chest and arms, a handsome sacred thread, rosary and deerskin. Sage’s cloth covers his waist, two quivers are tied there, and he carries bow, arrows and a splendid axe on his shoulder. His guise is peaceful but his deeds severe: indescribable in form, he seems heroic fury itself wearing a sage’s body as he enters among the kings.",
